lessons.notes:
## if / else

```c
if (condition) {
    // runs when condition is true (non-zero)
} else if (other) {
    // runs when the first was false and this is true
} else {
    // runs when all were false
}
```

## Comparisons and logic

| Operator | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| `==` / `!=` | equal / not equal |
| `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` | ordering |
| `&&` | and |
| `\|\|` | or |
| `!` | not |

A comparison gives `1` (true) or `0` (false). `&&` and `||` **short-circuit**: in `b != 0 && a / b > 2`, the division never runs when `b` is 0.

## Common bug: = vs ==

```c
if (x = 5)   // assigns 5, always true! (bug)
if (x == 5)  // compares (correct)
```

## Braces

Braces are optional for a single statement, but always using them avoids mistakes when you add lines later.

## switch

```c
switch (day) {
    case 6:
    case 7:
        printf("Weekend\n");
        break;
    default:
        printf("Weekday\n");
}
```

- Works with integers and `char`, not strings or doubles.
- `break` exits the switch. Leaving it out makes cases **fall through** - useful only when several cases share code, as above.

## The ternary operator

`int max = (a > b) ? a : b;` is a compact if/else that produces a value.

