lessons.notes:
## while

```c
while (condition) {
    body;
}
```
The condition is checked **before** each iteration. If it is false at the start, the body never runs.

## for

```c
for (init; condition; step) {
    body;
}
```
Equivalent to:
```c
init;
while (condition) {
    body;
    step;
}
```
Declaring the counter inside (`for (int i = 0; ...)`) keeps it local to the loop.

## do-while

```c
int choice;
do {
    printf("1) Play  0) Quit\n");
    scanf("%d", &choice);
} while (choice != 0);
```
The body runs at least once.

## Patterns

- **Counting**: `for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)` runs exactly `n` times.
- **Accumulating**: `sum += x;` inside the loop.
- **Counting matches**: `if (x % 2 == 0) evens++;`
- **Counting down**: `for (int i = 10; i >= 1; i--)`

## Off-by-one errors

`i < n` versus `i <= n` is the most common loop bug. Check the first and last values by hand: does the loop run for both?

## Avoid infinite loops

Make sure the body moves toward the end condition. Watch out for a stray semicolon: `while (i < 5);` loops forever on an empty body.

