lessons.notes:
## break and continue

- `break;` exits the innermost loop (or switch) immediately.
- `continue;` jumps to the next iteration (in a `for`, the step `i++` still runs).

Use them to keep code flat and readable, but do not overuse them.

## Nested loops

```c
for (int row = 1; row <= 9; row++) {
    for (int col = 1; col <= 9; col++) {
        printf("%3d", row * col);
    }
    printf("\n");
}
```
The inner loop runs fully for each outer iteration: 9 x 9 = 81 prints.

## Reading an unknown amount of input

```c
int x, count = 0;
long long sum = 0;
while (scanf("%d", &x) == 1) {
    count++;
    sum += x;
}
```

`scanf` returns the number of values converted. At end of input it returns `EOF` (a negative number). If it meets text that is not a number it returns 0, which also stops this loop.

## Max and min

```c
int max = 0;
int first = 1;
while (scanf("%d", &x) == 1) {
    if (first || x > max) max = x;
    first = 0;
}
```

Initialize from the first item (or use `INT_MIN` from `<limits.h>`). Starting at 0 is a bug when all numbers are negative.

## Flags

A *flag* variable like `found = 0` records whether something happened inside a loop, so you can act after the loop ends.

