lessons.notes:
## Address-of and dereference

| Syntax | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `int *p;` | `p` is a pointer to `int` |
| `&x` | address of `x` |
| `*p` | the value at the address in `p` |
| `p = NULL;` | `p` points to nothing |

```c
int x = 5;
int *p = &x;
*p = 7;          // x is now 7
printf("%p\n", (void *)p);  // prints the address
```

## Output parameters

C passes by value, so to "return" several results, pass pointers:

```c
void min_max(const int a[], int n, int *mn, int *mx) {
    *mn = *mx = a[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        if (a[i] < *mn) *mn = a[i];
        if (a[i] > *mx) *mx = a[i];
    }
}
// usage: min_max(arr, n, &lo, &hi);
```

## Pointer arithmetic

```c
int a[3] = {4, 5, 6};
int *p = a;        // same as &a[0]
printf("%d\n", *(p + 2));  // 6
```

`p + 1` advances by `sizeof(int)` bytes. Only move within an array (or one past its end).

## const and pointers

`const int *p` - you cannot change the value through `p`. Use it for read-only parameters.

## Rules for safe pointers

1. Initialize every pointer (to a valid address or `NULL`).
2. Check for `NULL` before dereferencing.
3. Never return the address of a local variable - it dies when the function returns.
4. Stay within array bounds.

