lessons.notes:
## Declaring a struct

```c
struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};
struct Point p = {3, 4};
```

With `typedef` you can drop the `struct` keyword:

```c
typedef struct {
    int x, y;
} Point;

Point p = {.x = 3, .y = 4};  // designated initializers
```

## Accessing members

- Variable: `p.x`
- Pointer: `ptr->x` (same as `(*ptr).x`)

## Functions and structs

```c
double dist2(const Point *a, const Point *b) {
    int dx = a->x - b->x, dy = a->y - b->y;
    return dx * dx + dy * dy;
}
```

Passing by pointer is efficient. `const` documents that the function will not modify it.

Returning a struct by value is fine for small structs:

```c
Point make_point(int x, int y) {
    Point p = {x, y};
    return p;
}
```

## Arrays of structs

```c
typedef struct { char name[32]; int score; } Player;
Player team[10];
team[0].score = 50;
```

## Nesting

Structs can contain other structs and arrays:

```c
typedef struct { Point top_left; Point bottom_right; } Rect;
```

## Comparing

You cannot use `==` on structs. Compare member by member, or write an `equals` function.

