lessons.notes:
## FILE pointers

```c
#include <stdio.h>
FILE *f = fopen("data.txt", "r");
if (!f) { perror("data.txt"); return 1; }
/* ... */
fclose(f);
```

## Modes

| Mode | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `"r"` | read (file must exist) |
| `"w"` | write (creates or **erases**) |
| `"a"` | append to end |
| `"r+"` | read and write |
| `"rb"`, `"wb"` | binary versions |

## Reading and writing

| Text | Function |
|------|----------|
| formatted write | `fprintf(f, "%d\n", x)` |
| formatted read | `fscanf(f, "%d", &x)` |
| line read | `fgets(buf, size, f)` |
| char | `fgetc(f)`, `fputc(c, f)` |
| binary | `fread`, `fwrite` |

## Standard streams

`stdin`, `stdout` and `stderr` are `FILE *` values that are always open. Errors should go to `stderr`:

```c
fprintf(stderr, "error: cannot open %s\n", path);
```

## Write stream-agnostic code

```c
int count_lines(FILE *in) {
    char buf[1024];
    int n = 0;
    while (fgets(buf, sizeof buf, in)) n++;
    return n;
}
// count_lines(stdin) or count_lines(fopen(...))
```

## End of file

`fgets` returns `NULL`, `fscanf` and `fgetc` return `EOF`. Do not use `feof` as a loop condition; check the read result instead.

## Note for this course

The runner gives your program standard input instead of real files, so exercises read from `stdin`. The same functions work on files you open yourself.

