lessons.notes:
## The classic C bugs

| Bug | Example | Prevention |
|-----|---------|------------|
| Buffer overflow | `strcpy(small, long_text)` | `snprintf`, width limits |
| Out-of-bounds | `a[n]` | loop with `i < n` |
| Use after free | `free(p); p[0] = 1;` | set `p = NULL` after free |
| Double free | `free(p); free(p);` | single owner |
| Uninitialized read | `int x; printf("%d", x);` | always initialize |
| Integer overflow | `int big = 50000 * 50000;` | wider types, check limits |
| Format bug | `printf(user_text)` | `printf("%s", user_text)` |

## Robust number parsing

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <errno.h>

int parse_int(const char *s, long *out) {
    char *end;
    errno = 0;
    long v = strtol(s, &end, 10);
    if (end == s || *end != '\0' || errno == ERANGE) return 0;
    *out = v;
    return 1;
}
```

- `end == s` - no digits at all.
- `*end != '\0'` - extra characters after the number.
- `errno == ERANGE` - too large or too small.

## Safe string building

```c
char msg[64];
snprintf(msg, sizeof msg, "Hello, %s! You are %d.", name, age);
```
`snprintf` never writes past `sizeof msg` and always adds `'\0'`.

## Tooling

```
gcc -Wall -Wextra -Werror -g -fsanitize=address,undefined main.c
```
AddressSanitizer stops the program at the exact line of an overflow or use-after-free and prints a report.

## Modern C

C23 (the current standard) adds `nullptr`, `bool` as a keyword, and `constexpr`. Many teams also follow guidelines like **CERT C** or **MISRA C** for safety-critical code.

